<body>
<p>Reports request matchers in an <code>authorizeHttpRequests</code> / <code>authorizeRequests</code> chain that are
  unreachable because an <b>earlier, more general matcher already covers them</b>.</p>

<p>Spring Security evaluates matchers in declaration order and the <b>first matching rule wins</b>, so a specific rule
  placed after a more general one that also matches it is silently ignored. More specific matchers must come first.</p>

<p><b>Incorrect</b> (the marked rules are ignored):</p>
<pre><code>
  http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
    .requestMatchers("/", "/home").permitAll()
    .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/home").hasRole("USER")        // covered by the earlier "/home"
    .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/test1/**").hasRole("USER")
    .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/test1/test2").hasRole("ADMIN") // covered by the earlier "/test1/**"
    .anyRequest().authenticated()
  );
</code></pre>

<p><b>Correct</b> (most specific first):</p>
<pre><code>
  http.authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
    .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/home").hasRole("USER")
    .requestMatchers("/", "/home").permitAll()
    .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/test1/test2").hasRole("ADMIN")
    .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, "/test1/**").hasRole("USER")
    .anyRequest().authenticated()
  );
</code></pre>
</body>
</html>
